```java
public class Expression {
    private String value;

    public Expression(String value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public String getValue() {
        return value;
    }
}

public abstract class Node {
    public abstract Object accept(NodeVisitor visitor);
}

public class BinaryExpression extends Expression implements Node {
    protected Expression left;
    protected Expression right;
    protected char operator;

    public BinaryExpression(Expression left, Expression right, char operator) {
        this.left = left;
        this.right = right;
        this.operator = operator;
    }

    @Override
    public Object accept(NodeVisitor visitor) {
        return visitor.visit(this);
    }
}

public interface NodeVisitor {
    Object visit(BinaryExpression expression);
}

public class NumberLiteral extends Expression implements Node {
    private int value;

    public NumberLiteral(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    @Override
    public Object accept(NodeVisitor visitor) {
        return value;
    }
}

public class BinaryOperatorVisitor implements NodeVisitor {
    private String operator;

    public BinaryOperatorVisitor(String operator) {
        this.operator = operator;
    }

    @Override
    public Object visit(BinaryExpression expression) {
        if (expression.getOperator() == operator.charAt(0)) {
            return new NumberLiteral((int) ((NumberLiteral) expression.getLeft().getValue()).getValue() + (int) ((NumberLiteral) expression.getRight().getValue()));
        } else {
            throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Unsupported operator");
        }
    }

    @Override
    public Object visit(Expression value) {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Unsupported node type");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Expression left = new NumberLiteral(5);
        Expression right = new NumberLiteral(3);
        Expression expression = new BinaryExpression(left, right, '+');
        NodeVisitor visitor = new BinaryOperatorVisitor("+");

        Object result = expression.accept(visitor);
        System.out.println(result);  // Output: 8
    }
}
```

In this solution, we define an abstract `Node` class and a concrete `BinaryExpression` class that extends it. The `BinaryExpression` class represents a binary expression with two operands (left and right) and an operator.

We also define an interface `NodeVisitor` that has a method for visiting each node type. In this case, we create a `BinaryOperatorVisitor` class that implements the `NodeVisitor` interface. This visitor knows how to visit the `BinaryExpression` nodes and perform the required operation based on the operator.

Finally, in the `main` method, we create an instance of `BinaryExpression`, an instance of `NumberLiteral`, and a visitor object of type `BinaryOperatorVisitor`. We then use the `accept` method of the `BinaryExpression` object to apply the visitor to it, which performs the required operation.
